{"url": "https://flora.insead.edu/fichiersti_wp/inseadwp2013/2013-110.pdf", "title": "Insead Faculty & Research Working Paper", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "(HRL). The coverage of an article in RavenPack increases the speed of reaction by 1.3 percentage points or 10% relative to the mean in the case of absolute returns and by 0.5 percentage points or 9%\n...\nNext, we focus on stock returns and ask whether the market reaction to RavenPack is “directional”. We find that the sentiment direction of an article as determined by RavenPack predicts the stock price reaction to the article better, when RavenPack consistently identifies the article as having high relevance. The difference in stock price reaction between HRH and HRL articles is 0.5 basis points during the first 5 seconds after the announcement, or approximately 19.5% per day. The effect is almost identical in the case of market-adjusted returns and industry-adjusted returns. Again, a placebo test does not show a statistically significant effect during the period before RavenPack went live.\n...\nWe want to study how news analytics provided by RavenPack affect the way the market reacts to news wires. As a first step, we examine whether the market reaction to news is faster after RavenPack was introduced in April 2009. For this purpose, we focus only on articles that are reported as highly relevant in both versions (HRH) and compare the market reaction for these articles in the time before and after RavenPack went live. We study the reaction in terms of cumulative returns within the first 120 seconds after an article. We multiply returns by the sentiment direction to be able to c", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 623380, "excerpt": "%PDF-1.6 %���� 11135 0 obj >stream 9�/ b�'�����&F8焦^޺[��:�۸ \u0011���\u001b��|M]�I܇d�?��\u0018\u0001�\u0002F�S��ͫB\u0012�g�����0�i!\u0013. >stream ����:��;sM�fY���R���\u0003uB F\u001a�P.� � ��G\b� \bw�\"���FRK��O��Pz�b_�n;����R?�GtLQh�\bf��s�C��J�\\3�O�kp8���&�\u0006�zt�^�1Pr�b�[\"�+�2I0X* 9��|���~m\u0007d�9�\u000e�ҩ�=a���e���O >stream E��>�6�\b\u000f���K9\u0010\u0018�2e��\u0013����[&\u0006\u001aCy̰TT�F_�9I�{�[{\u0005�E�9�kܛD XK���\u0007;��D�r�\u0001�2�����}�ً@i� ����H>V��kW� A \"x� �\u0011��\u0006m�r�\u0016�v�Ϣv� �)\u0004M��\u0004", "jev": {"concrete": 0.24, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.95, "market": "other", "us_long_only": 0.28, "recent": 0.03, "code": 0.04}, "priority": 0.012}
{"url": "https://mlquants.substack.com/p/stocks-macro-announcement-incremental", "title": "Stock’s Macro Announcement Incremental Information and Intraday Momentum", "engine": "exa", "published": "2025-09-14", "snippet": "### Intraday momentum effect strengthened by macro announcements, driven by institutional investors.\n...\nThis paper investigates the relationship between intraday momentum and macroeconomic announcements from an information perspective. The authors employ the difference in abnormal return variance (DVAR) to measure the incremental information of individual stocks before and after macro announcements, which is not fully captured by the six-factor asset pricing model. The results show that the intraday momentum effect is stronger during fiscal policy, monetary policy, and high-level meeting announcements. Further analysis reveals that such incremental information primarily drives intraday momentum through institutional investors' non-high-frequency portfolio adjustments. In contrast, retail investors' late-informed trading behavior is weakened, which diminishes the intraday momentum effect. The paper uses high-frequency trading data from the Wind database and macro-level announcement incremental information (DVAR) data from the CSMAR database to construct a comprehensive dataset. The authors also control for stock-specific characteristic variables, such as stock liquidity, stock turnover, stock size, and book-to-market ratio. The findings of this paper have important implications for investors and policymakers, as they highlight the role of macroeconomic announcements in shaping intraday momentum. Overall, this paper provides new insights into the intraday momentum effect and c", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 2745, "excerpt": "Paper Metadata - Publication Date: 2025-08-27 - Source: SSRN - Link: https://papers.ssrn.com/sol3/papers.cfm?abstract_id=5413265 Keywords - News Alpha - Intraday momentum Notes for Review This paper investigates the relationship between intraday momentum and macroeconomic announcements from an information perspective. The authors employ the difference in abnormal return variance (DVAR) to measure ", "jev": {"concrete": 0.11, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.49, "market": "china_a_shares", "us_long_only": 0.33, "recent": 0.49, "code": 0.08}, "priority": 0.007}
{"url": "https://www.frbsf.org/wp-content/uploads/wp2025-30.pdf", "title": "Financial Market Effects of FOMC Communication: Evidence from a New Event-Study Database", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "rate expectations caused by the statement release. By contrast, while still informative about\n...\nthe policy path, FOMC meeting minutes generate much less market volatility: Most relevant \npolicy information appears to be communicated effectively via the FOMC statements and \nthe Chair’s press conferences. As a practical matter, we therefore recommend the monetary\n...\nMonetary policy event studies rely on the assumption that changes in asset prices during tight windows around policy announcements are predominantly driven by the revealed policy news. If the event windows are too wide, many other factors may affect asset prices, making statistical inference more difficult. By contrast, if the windows are too narrow, market participants may not have enough time to fully digest the news, implying that prices may not fully incorporate the new information. For FOMC statements, we follow common practice since G¨urkaynak et al. (2005) and use 30-minute windows. Our “statement window” therefore starts 10 minutes before and ends 20 minutes after the release of the statement. For FOMC meeting minutes, we use the same approach as for FOMC statements. While the minutes are much longer than the FOMC statements, they are also released to reporters under embargo, and the available evidence suggests rapid price discovery (Rosa, 2013). The “minutes window” is the 30-minute interval that starts 10 minutes before and ends 20 minutes after the release of the minutes. The post-meeting press confere", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 312960, "excerpt": "%PDF-1.7 %���� 1071 0 obj > endobj 1089 0 obj >/Filter/FlateDecode/ID[ ]/Index[1071 41]/Info 1070 0 R/Length 95/Prev 643633/Root 1072 0 R/Size 1112/Type/XRef/W[1 3 1]>>stream h�bbd`\u0010``b``y \"\u0019��I=