{"url": "https://ideas.repec.org/p/cpr/ceprdp/10534.html", "title": "First to ?Read? the News: News Analytics and Institutional Trading", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "We investigate whether providers of high frequency news analytics affect the stock market. As identification, we exploit a unique experiment based on differences in news event classifications between different product releases of a major provider of news analytics. We document a causal effect of news analytics on the market, irrespective of the informational content of the news. Coverage in news analytics speeds up the market reaction in terms of stock price response and trading volume, and increases illiquidity immediately after the article. Furthermore, we document that traders learn dynamically about the precision of news analytics.\n\n---", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 15053, "excerpt": "First to ?Read? the News: News Analytics and Institutional Trading Download full text from publisher References listed on IDEAS - Alan S. Gerber & Dean Karlan & Daniel Bergan, 2009. \"Does the Media Matter? A Field Experiment Measuring the Effect of Newspapers on Voting Behavior and Political Opinions,\" American Economic Journal: Applied Economics, American Economic Association, vol. 1(2), pages 35", "jev": {"concrete": 0.09, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.95, "market": "other", "us_long_only": 0.36, "recent": 0.05, "code": 0.07}, "priority": 0.005}
{"url": "https://ideas.repec.org/a/eee/finmar/v17y2014icp121-149.html", "title": "Intraday liquidity dynamics and news releases around price jumps: Evidence from the DJIA stocks", "engine": "exa", "published": "N/A", "snippet": "We study the dynamics of liquidity and news releases around jumps by identifying their intraday timing for the Dow Jones Industrial Average index constituents. Jumps are found to coincide with a significant increase in trading costs and demand for immediacy, amplified by the release of news. Liquidity supply remains nevertheless high and there is strong evidence of resilience. Liquidity shocks in the effective spread and the number of trades are the key drivers behind the occurrence of a jump. Order imbalance appears to be the most informative liquidity variable with respect to price discovery, especially after the arrival of news.\n\n---", "extra": {}, "read_via": "direct", "text_chars": 17926, "excerpt": "Intraday liquidity dynamics and news releases around price jumps: Evidence from the DJIA stocks Download full text from publisher As the access to this document is restricted, you may want to look for a different version below or for a different version of it. Other versions of this item: - Boudt, Kris & Petitjean, Mikael, 2014. \"Intraday liquidity dynamics and news releases around price jumps: Ev", "jev": {"concrete": 0.04, "evidence": "none", "evidence_confidence": 0.99, "market": "us_stocks_etfs", "us_long_only": 0.37, "recent": 0.02, "code": 0.05}, "priority": 0.004}
